ary is struggling to push back the  taliban  like here in afghanistan's northwest  peace talks between the government and  the militants have so far  failed and many fear the violence will  only get worse  once the last u.s soldiers have returned  home  let's explore this with adam weinstein  who is a research fellow at the quincy  institute for responsible statecraft  he's an  expert on afghanistan where he also  served as a us marine  he joins us from washington welcome to  dw  um well let's start with the the the big  question then pulling us troops uh  out of afghanistan regardless of the  conditions on the ground was that the  right decision  well i think uh conditions-based  withdrawal would  have um inevitably left u.s troops on  the ground indefinitely  so it was the right call because it  recognizes the reality  that the only solution to the war in  afghanistan is a political settlement  and that cannot be imposed by foreign  troops  and when do you think in that 20 years  that realization  finally sank in  well i i think among some uh parts of  government and  and certainly outside of government the  realization sank in uh years and years  ago  uh but i think uh both president trump  and president biden recognized  the pulse of the country in the united  states was that they didn't want to see  an extension  to u.s military involvement in this war  and  i think the slow progress in  intra-afghan negotiations  really hammered home the fact that a  political settlement  was unlikely to happen and  it couldn't happen with the presence of  foreign troops because  that is one of the drivers of the  conflict in the first place  but you seem to be saying that one of  the main things that that changed  was the appetite of the american public  for this  uh this conflict  well i think the appetite changed but um  you know  the american public might have been  ahead of of the us government in  realizing the futility of this conflict  even if  the american public are not experts on  afghanistan  um when you were serving as a marine in  afghanistan were you were you  clear about why you were there  i think at the time i served i i was  young i was a us marine  i served in a ruseguan uh province and  i probably did have a bit of an  ideological  view as to why we were there i did feel  that we were  um preventing terrorism from reaching  the united states and we were engaging  in nation-building  activities that were admirable but um  the reality is that  that mission was it it was not  accomplished and there was a failure of  u.s  policy and frankly nato policy in in  afghanistan  it doesn't mean that nato countries  should be disengaged economically or  diplomatically  but there simply is not a military  solution to the conflict that can be  imposed  from the outside and so when i  as i did today i read an interview with  taliban's  shadow mayor in the bulk district of  afghanistan  and he said we have won them war and  america  has lost is he wrong  well the taliban have won in the sense  that  uh one of their primary goals was was  for nato troops to  to leave but i think the taliban are  going to find that they're now  part of a more intensified uh civil war  and the only real solution  that allows everyone to win as a  political settlement so the taliban may  feel they've won  but uh in reality uh they haven't  and i don't think taliban statements  about winning or losing  should dictate uh u.s policy or nato  policy  no not so much that but but just just  being clear about  what was achieved and what wasn't  because there does seem to have been  some some sort of muddying of the waters  president biden saying  well we're not there to nation build and  as as you said you were  when you were there as a young man  taking part in this well that was  clearly  part of what you were what you were sold  um  i wonder how the afghans then feel  because are afghans wrong to think that  the united states has  abandoned them  i understand why afghans feel that way  and some of that failure  is the u.s messaging the reality is that  president biden is in a difficult  position and he's correct that the  original intention  of invading afghanistan was to counter  terrorism and we have degraded  albeit not defeated al qaeda but  i think the united states and nato  partners have to grapple with the  reality that  the war became a nation-building  exercise and we did sell it to afghans  um and to uh the random pile of the us  uh you know the u.s soldiers and and to  the broader public as a nation building  exercise so we have to grapple with that  failure  um i mean i i think the german public  was  where it was supportive of uh you know  in military involvement in afghanistan  some of that did have to do with  advancing human rights and nation  building  and so that was um that that record is  something that the united states is  going to have to grapple with  even if president biden didn't do it in  his speech  and one of america's allies after 9 11  president bush triggered article 5 of  the north atlantic treaty  the united states has been attacked and  its allies rallied to its aid to go  after al-qaeda in afghanistan  so aren't those same allies now right to  feel aggrieved at the way president  trump  began the u.s pull-out without  consulting them and then president biden  set a date for a full withdrawal  regardless of conditions on the ground  well like i said i think a full  withdrawal that was not condition based  was the only  uh the only sustainable forgive me for  interrupting  the time is sure you you've said that  i'm wondering about the attitude of  america's allies  america in this instance appears to be  an unreliable ally  well this is the problem with  interventionist policies on the part of  the united states  the united states forgets that all of  these other countries have their own  domestic politics  a perfect example was the failure to  communicate with germany  about what the withdrawal timetable  would be at the same time that germany  had to  uh seek an extension for its mandate in  afghanistan  um from its own government and from its  own representatives  i mean that's a that's a case in point  of of how the united states was an  unreliable partner in afghanistan  but that's because these interventionist  uh foreign policies uh inherently  inherently create partnerships that are  dictated by the united states  and i think we also have to remember  that u.s  society was fundamentally um  trans transformed by the war on terror  in a way that wasn't true in europe in  my opinion  so i i i would ask our european partners  to understand the reasons for the us  withdrawal  but i certain i certainly wouldn't  excuse the lack of communication  um and the limbo that the trump  administration in particular  put uh nato partners in good talking  thank you so much for joining us uh adam  weinstein from the quincy institute for  responsible statecraft  thanks for having me  now in recent years the country with the  second largest troop deployment  in afghanistan after the americans was  germany which has also  completed the withdrawal of its forces  59  german soldiers died in the mission  a memorial to the dead the forest of  remembrance as the bundeswehr calls it  it's dedicated to the men and women of  the military who lost their lives in the  course of duty  stefan gone is a guide at the memorial  site which was set up in 2014  many years after the start of the  mission in afghanistan  tradition a culture of remembrance and  the use of memorials can't arise  from one day to the next  they have to grow the culture of  remembrance within the armed forces  grows as the mission progresses  and with the experiences of the soldiers  on that mission  um this culture of remembrance it is  growing  but most people in germany have never  even heard of the forest of remembrance  which is far from the capital germans  don't feel particularly close  to their country's military the true  withdrawal from afghanistan is a point  in case  when the soldiers landed in germany  there was no politician to greet them  days later the country is still  discussing whether and if so  how tribute should be paid to the  mission another controversial point is  the treatment of afghans who worked for  the german mission on the ground  translators for instance they want the  bundeswehr to guarantee their safety  since the withdrawal they fear  retribution by the taliban  i'm told that the hunt for allies of the  west has already started in maasai  sharif  we hear people in the neighborhood are  being taken away by the taliban  our local assistants feel they have to  go into hiding  marcus groshan himself served in  afghanistan  now he lobbies for afghans who assisted  the mission to be brought to germany  but the federal government is demanding  that they pay for their own flights  german officials say the country has  issued 2  400 visas in all aspects of our mission  in afghanistan  we were assisted by dedicated and  competent afghans  both men and women who supported us  without them we would not have been able  to do the job we were sent there to do  we understand the responsibility we have  for these people  in the forest of remembrance there will  soon be another memorial grove  dedicated to the soldiers who fell in  mazari sharif  the city where many people who helped  the german mission are still waiting for  help  from germany
 

